#!/bin/sh
#
# Refuse a commit on master. Work lands there through staging and a pull request.
#
# Why a hook and not a rule: the rule already existed and was written down, and it
# was still broken twice in one session on 2026-08-20 — both times by the same
# slip, `git checkout master` to fast-forward after a merge and then simply not
# switching back. Nothing failed at the time. The commits were caught by eye and
# moved with `git branch -f`, which is luck wearing the appearance of a process.
#
# What it protects is not master's contents — GitHub's branch protection does that,
# and a push to master is refused there. It protects the *CI gate*: master requires
# a pull request, and the checks that gate it are the reason staging exists. A
# commit made on master is not wrong yet, it is merely in the wrong place, and the
# cost is only paid later — at the point where the work has to be untangled, or
# worse, where it is pushed with a lease nobody thinks about.
#
# Same shape as the lock on GitHub: not against the owner, against inattention.
# `git commit --no-verify` skips it, deliberately.
#
# Enable once per clone:
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks

set -u

branch=$(git symbolic-ref --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo "")

# A detached HEAD is not a branch and not this hook's business: bisecting,
# checking out a tag, and the worktrees used to run a test against an older
# commit all sit there legitimately.
[ -z "$branch" ] && exit 0

case "$branch" in
    master|main) ;;
    *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# A merge or a rebase in progress means git is driving, not a person: refusing
# here would leave the repository mid-operation with no obvious way forward.
git_dir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null || echo ".git")
if [ -e "$git_dir/MERGE_HEAD" ] || [ -d "$git_dir/rebase-merge" ] || [ -d "$git_dir/rebase-apply" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

printf '\n\033[31mcommit refused: you are on %s\033[0m\n' "$branch" >&2
cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Work lands on master through staging and a pull request — CI gates the merge,
and a commit made here has to be moved before it can go anywhere.

Move to staging, keeping the changes you have staged:

    git checkout -B staging

If commits are already sitting on master, they are not lost:

    git branch -f staging HEAD
    git branch -f master origin/master
    git checkout staging

To commit here anyway: git commit --no-verify

EOF
exit 1
